298	ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
299	=====================
300	
301	If an environment variable prefixed with `EZA_` is not set, for backward compatibility, it will default to its counterpart starting with `EXA_`.
302	
303	eza responds to the following environment variables:
304	
305	## `COLUMNS`
306	
307	Overrides the width of the terminal, in characters, however, `-w` takes precedence.
308	
309	For example, ‘`COLUMNS=80 eza`’ will show a grid view with a maximum width of 80 characters.
310	
311	This option won’t do anything when eza’s output doesn’t wrap, such as when using the `--long` view.
312	
313	## `EZA_STRICT`
314	
315	Enables _strict mode_, which will make eza error when two command-line options are incompatible.
316	
317	Usually, options can override each other going right-to-left on the command line, so that eza can be given aliases: creating an alias ‘`eza=eza --sort=ext`’ then running ‘`eza --sort=size`’ with that alias will run ‘`eza --sort=ext --sort=size`’, and the sorting specified by the user will override the sorting specified by the alias.
318	
319	In strict mode, the two options will not co-operate, and eza will error.
320	
321	This option is intended for use with automated scripts and other situations where you want to be certain you’re typing in the right command.
322	
323	## `EZA_GRID_ROWS`
324	
325	Limits the grid-details view (‘`eza --grid --long`’) so it’s only activated when at least the given number of rows of output would be generated.
326	
327	With widescreen displays, it’s possible for the grid to look very wide and sparse, on just one or two lines with none of the columns lining up.
328	By specifying a minimum number of rows, you can only use the view if it’s going to be worth using.
329	
330	## `EZA_ICON_SPACING`
331	
332	Specifies the number of spaces to print between an icon (see the ‘`--icons`’ option) and its file name.
333	
334	Different terminals display icons differently, as they usually take up more than one character width on screen, so there’s no “standard” number of spaces that eza can use to separate an icon from text. One space may place the icon too close to the text, and two spaces may place it too far away. So the choice is left up to the user to configure depending on their terminal emulator.
335	
336	## `NO_COLOR`
337	
338	Disables colours in the output (regardless of its value). Can be overridden by `--color` option.
339	
340	See `https://no-color.org/` for details.
341	
342	## `LS_COLORS`, `EZA_COLORS`
343	
344	Specifies the colour scheme used to highlight files based on their name and kind, as well as highlighting metadata and parts of the UI.
345	
346	For more information on the format of these environment variables, see the [eza_colors.5.md](eza_colors.5.md) manual page.
347	
348	## `EZA_OVERRIDE_GIT`
349	
350	Overrides any `--git` or `--git-repos` argument
351	
352	## `EZA_MIN_LUMINANCE`
353	Specifies the minimum luminance to use when color-scale is active. It's value can be between -100 to 100.
354	
355	## `EZA_ICONS_AUTO`
356	
357	If set, automates the same behavior as using `--icons` or `--icons=auto`. Useful for if you always want to have icons enabled.
358	
359	Any explicit use of the `--icons=WHEN` flag overrides this behavior.
360	
361	## `EZA_STDIN_SEPARATOR`
362	
363	Specifies the separator to use when file names are piped from stdin. Defaults to newline.
364	
365	## `EZA_CONFIG_DIR`
366	
367	Specifies the directory where eza will look for its configuration and theme files. Defaults to `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eza` or `$HOME/.config/eza` if `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is not set.
